INT. STARVE CREEK SALT WORKS - STAGE STOP - NIGHT

A low adobe room crusted with white. Salt in the walls, salt on the table, salt on the beams like frost. A cookstove ticks. A single lamp swings from a nail. Outside, wind drags the dust against the shutters.

PERPETUA OAKES, 50s, innkeeper, broad hands, apron grey with age, ladles stew into a tin bowl and sets it, without ceremony, in front of JUBAL CREED, 40s, a drifter in a sun-bleached duster, who has not taken his hat off or his eyes from the door. At the end of the table sits DEPUTY HENRY TASKER, 22, a badge pinned to a shirt too clean for this country. He pushes beans around a plate and tries not to look at the stranger's holster.

PERPETUA
Eat, Mr. Creed. The stew will not improve with watching.

JUBAL
Ma'am, I am watching the door, not the stew.

TASKER
Expecting someone?

JUBAL
Always.

TASKER
There's no one out there. Nearest town's forty miles.

JUBAL
Forty miles is a Tuesday.

Perpetua snorts, wipes her hands on her apron, pours coffee. Tasker clears his throat.

TASKER
Mr. Creed, I have to ask your business at Starve Creek.

JUBAL
Sleeping.

TASKER
In a salt works.

JUBAL
It's quiet.

PERPETUA
It's quiet because the last man who made noise is buried behind the privy.

TASKER
Mrs. Oakes!

PERPETUA
Well, he is.

The door bangs open. Wind and dust sweep through the room. The lamp lurches on its nail. In the doorway stands ISABELLE "BELLE" VANCE, 30s, a black leather coat dripping dust, a rifle in the crook of one arm and a folded paper in her free hand. She shuts the door with her boot. Perpetua stiffens. Tasker's chair scrapes back. Jubal doesn't move at all, but his hand has found the edge of the table.

BELLE
Evening, all.

PERPETUA
We're full.

BELLE
You're four. I counted the horses.

TASKER
Ma'am, this is a private table.

BELLE
Then I'll stand.

She unfolds the paper and lays it on the salt-crusted wood, face up, under the lamp. A wanted poster. A sketched face, hat low, jaw square.

BELLE (CONT'D)
Jubal Creed. Five hundred dollars. Mexican border, Santa Rita, the payroll coach.

JUBAL
You've mistaken me for a better-looking man.

BELLE
Mr. Creed, I tracked you through three counties. I don't need your face.

TASKER
Ma'am, I'm the law here.

BELLE
Deputy, you're twenty-two. Sit.

Tasker sits. Perpetua sets the ladle down very slowly. Belle regards Jubal across the table.

JUBAL
Miss Vance, I'll make you an offer.

BELLE
I don't take offers.

JUBAL
You'll take this one. There was no payroll coach. There was a man in the coach who needed to disappear, and I helped him. For three hundred dollars. He's in Sonora, teaching school.

BELLE
Whose man?

JUBAL
A judge's.

BELLE
Which judge?

JUBAL
The one who signed this poster.

Silence. The wind drops. The stove ticks. Belle looks at the poster as if for the first time. She turns it over. Reads the back.

BELLE
There's a seal.

JUBAL
There always is.

TASKER
That's the county seal.

PERPETUA
Henry, whose side are you on?

TASKER
I don't know, Mrs. Oakes.

BELLE
Deputy. Whose signature is on this?

TASKER
(small)
Judge Garrity's.

Belle folds the paper. Looks at Tasker, then Jubal, then the door.

BELLE
Mrs. Oakes, I believe I'll take that stew after all.

Perpetua ladles. Belle sits. Rifle on her knees. The four of them eat in a silence full of arithmetic.
